e in a PCR reaction can inhibit  amplification and this you can see here  in the lower gel image where when you  have the larger amounts of input you  don't get amplification of your band so  the amount of genomic DNA per PCR  reaction is 825 nano grams for 100%  amplification efficiency so for 6.6  micrograms of the input genomic DNA that  is required for 1,000 fold  representation you'll need to perform  eight PCR reactions per genomic DNA  sample we've also designed and tested  the PCR primers and optimized the PCR  conditions including the enzyme amount  the amount of genomic DNA inputs as I  just showed you whether or not you need  to add additives as well as the cycle  numbers for the PCR in order to amplify  shrnas and sgrna without an energy bias  so here what I'm showing you is the  result of the law counts obtained from  next-gen sequencing after PCR  amplification with using fusion  polymerase and using the optimized PCR  primers and conditions as you can see  the PCR primers do not show an shrna  specific energy bias and we obtain  equivalent law counts regardless of the  free energy which is good  okay so earlier I mentioned that the  size of the shRNA or the sgrna pool has  an impact on the practical aspects to  performing an effective screen the  widest pool size matter well in order to  have each cell expressing a single  construct in order to ensure that the  knockdown or the knock out of a single  gene in a particular cell is responsible  for the resulting phenotype we recommend  performing transduction at a low mo eye  of 0.3 or less now using the Poisson  distribution this results in  approximately 22% of cells having a  single integrins now in order to  determine how many cells are needed for  a specific cool you multiply the number  of constructs in the lentiviral pool by  the desired fold representation and this  then gives you the desired number of  cells with lentiviral integrations now  you then need to take into account the  Poisson distri